Rules
Id	Rule
M1	The input is an OTLP JSON Lines file: UTF-8, one JSON value per line, each line a TracesData, MetricsData or LogsData in OTLP JSON encoding, one kind of data per file. The eight are recorded on spans and on events, so TracesData and LogsData carry them and MetricsData none.
M2	The lines of a file are in no guaranteed order. They are read in the file's order, and nothing is sorted.
M3	A receiver ignores fields with unknown names.
M4	Trace and span ids are hex strings, case-insensitive. They are written as the text the input holds, and compared without regard to case.
M5	Each of the eight is found by its attribute key: gen_ai.system_instructions, gen_ai.tool.definitions, gen_ai.input.messages, gen_ai.output.messages, gen_ai.tool.call.arguments, gen_ai.tool.call.result, gen_ai.memory.records and gen_ai.retrieval.documents. On events it is structured; on spans it is structured, or a JSON string where structure is not supported. Both forms are read.
M6	Each of the eight has sheets of its own. Input messages and output messages are kept apart, each with its own sheets.
M7	Every row of a relation draws on the same domains, so a schema definition with fields of its own is a sheet of its own: each part type, each tool type and each record type. A part or tool belongs to the definition its type value names; one whose type names no definition is generic.
M8	A nonsimple field is struck from its parent and becomes a child sheet that carries its parent's key; normalization applies because the nesting is a tree and no key is nonsimple. A child row's pointer extends its parent's pointer, so the parent's key is carried in it.
M9	Every row is keyed by traceId and spanId, the span or event it came from, and pointer, a JSON Pointer from the root of the attribute value to the row's value.
M10	A value of any shape is one sheet with a row per node: its pointer, its type (object, array, string, number, true, false or null) and its value. The value of a string is its text, of a number its text as written; an object, an array, true, false and null have an empty value. Rows are in the value's own order: a node before the nodes within it, members and elements as written.
M11	The rows of a relation are unordered, while a JSON array is ordered and input messages are in the order sent. A JSON Pointer names an array element by its zero-based index, so order is kept in the pointer.
M12	An enum (role, modality, finish_reason) holds a simple, atomic value, so it is a column.
M13	Every object of the eight allows further properties. A property beyond its schema goes to its object's additionalProperties sheet, a node sheet named with the schemas' own keyword.
M14	An implementation may limit the range and precision of numbers, so a number is written as the input writes it.
M15	A field that holds HT, LF, FF or CR cannot be represented, and a generator does not write it: its field is left empty.
M16	Names are the sources' own: traceId and spanId as OTLP JSON writes them, pointer for the JSON Pointer, type and value for a JSON value, and each schema's field names and definitions. A sheet's name is its parent's name, '.', and the field or definition it comes from; a definition is named by the type value its data carries.
M17	A log record carries a trace id and span id only optionally, and one without them is not associated with a trace: its traceId and spanId are empty, and its rows are told apart only by their order in the file (M2).
M18	The output is one MTSV file: every sheet of the Sheets, in their order, each with its header, and no records where the input holds none.
M19	A field is text, and the optional fields of the eight default to null. Null, an absent field and the empty string are written as an empty field, and an empty field reads as null.
